2025-05-01 03:22:36 - Thread-1365 (execute) - INFO - [Exploration]
Relevant columns and their potential usage  
• Movie.year : film release year (needed to build per–actor timelines)  
• Movie.MID : movie key for joining to cast data  
• M_Cast.PID : person (actor) identifier in each movie credit  
• M_Cast.MID : movie identifier to reach its year  
• Person.PID : joins a credit to the actor’s demographic row  
• Person.Name : actor’s name (for human‑readable results)

Typical join condition  
Movie.MID = M_Cast.MID  
AND Person.PID = M_Cast.PID  

Typical filter condition  
Movie.year IS NOT NULL ‑‑ keep rows that own a valid year value  


Below are ten exploratory SQL statements (each different) that surface the data you would need in order to decide whether an actor ever experiences a four‑year gap without a film credit.  
All queries are read‑only, use DISTINCT when helpful, and cap their output with LIMIT 20.


```sql
-- 1) Show 20 actors in the database
SELECT DISTINCT
       p."PID",
       TRIM(p."Name") AS "actor_name"
FROM "Person" AS p
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2) List 20 movies with their year of release
SELECT
       m."MID",
       m."title",
       m."year"
FROM "Movie" AS m
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3) First 20 cast links: which actor appears in which film
SELECT
       c."PID",
       c."MID"
FROM "M_Cast" AS c
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4) Combine cast credits with the movie table to see the year for each credit
SELECT
       c."PID",
       c."MID",
       m."year"
FROM "M_Cast" AS c
JOIN "Movie" AS m  ON m."MID" = c."MID"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5) Pick one sample actor and list all the calendar years in which they had a credit
SELECT DISTINCT
       m."year"
FROM "Person"  AS p
JOIN "M_Cast"  AS c ON c."PID" = p."PID"
JOIN "Movie"   AS m ON m."MID" = c."MID"
WHERE p."PID" = (SELECT "PID" FROM "Person" LIMIT 1)   -- sample actor
ORDER BY m."year"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6) For every actor, show their earliest and latest movie year
SELECT
       c."PID",
       MIN(CAST(m."year" AS INTEGER)) AS "first_year",
       MAX(CAST(m."year" AS INTEGER)) AS "last_year"
FROM "M_Cast" AS c
JOIN "Movie"  AS m ON m."MID" = c."MID"
GROUP BY c."PID"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7) Count how many movie appearances each actor has made
SELECT
       c."PID",
       COUNT(*) AS "num_credits"
FROM "M_Cast" AS c
GROUP BY c."PID"
ORDER BY "num_credits" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8) List every actor‑year pair (deduplicated) — the basic unit for gap analysis
SELECT DISTINCT
       c."PID",
       CAST(m."year" AS INTEGER) AS "yr"
FROM "M_Cast" AS c
JOIN "Movie"  AS m ON m."MID" = c."MID"
ORDER BY c."PID", "yr"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9) For one actor, display each year and the next chronological credit year
SELECT
       a."PID",
       a."yr"          AS "current_year",
       MIN(b."yr")     AS "next_year"
FROM
     (SELECT DISTINCT c."PID", CAST(m."year" AS INTEGER) AS "yr"
      FROM "M_Cast" AS c
      JOIN "Movie"  AS m ON m."MID" = c."MID") AS a
LEFT JOIN
     (SELECT DISTINCT c."PID", CAST(m."year" AS INTEGER) AS "yr"
      FROM "M_Cast" AS c
      JOIN "Movie"  AS m ON m."MID" = c."MID") AS b
  ON a."PID" = b."PID" AND b."yr" > a."yr"
WHERE a."PID" = (SELECT "PID" FROM "Person" LIMIT 1)   -- sample actor
GROUP BY a."PID", a."yr"
ORDER BY a."yr"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10) Identify actors who, somewhere in their career, experience a gap ≥ 4 years
-- (negating this set later will tell us the count requested in the problem)
SELECT DISTINCT
       g."PID"
FROM (
      SELECT
             a."PID",
             MIN(b."yr") - a."yr" AS "gap"
      FROM  /* reuse the actor‑year CTE from query 9 */
           (SELECT DISTINCT c."PID", CAST(m."year" AS INTEGER) AS "yr"
            FROM "M_Cast" AS c
            JOIN "Movie"  AS m ON m."MID" = c."MID") AS a
      JOIN
           (SELECT DISTINCT c."PID", CAST(m."year" AS INTEGER) AS "yr"
            FROM "M_Cast" AS c
            JOIN "Movie"  AS m ON m."MID" = c."MID") AS b
        ON a."PID" = b."PID" AND b."yr" > a."yr"
      GROUP BY a."PID", a."yr"
      HAVING MIN(b."yr") - a."yr" >= 4
     ) AS g
LIMIT 20;
```

These queries progressively reveal all information necessary to compute the final answer (“how many actors never have such a four‑year gap”), culminating in query 10, whose result set represents the violators.  
Counting the actors not in that set would yield the requested total.
